// WorktreeMatchesHEAD reports whether the working tree's indexable content is
// byte-identical to the committed HEAD tree.
//
// It is the precondition that lets a caller reuse a tree-keyed index cache for
// a working-tree query: when nothing is modified, staged, deleted or untracked,
// every file a working-tree walk can see is exactly the file HEAD records, so a
// snapshot built from one is a snapshot of the other.
//
// The check is deliberately conservative:
//
//   - it compares HEAD to the index by raw mode and object ID, then hashes
//     tracked worktree bytes itself and compares them to the index object ID;
//   - it lists every untracked-but-not-ignored file, because a working-tree walk
//     indexes those files too;
//   - files git ignores are excluded from BOTH sides (the provider's
//     working-tree walk honours .gitignore), so an ignored build artifact
//     changing cannot invalidate the equality. If the ignore rules themselves
//     change so that a file stops being ignored, that file becomes untracked
//     and this check turns false, which is the safe direction;
//   - raw hashing intentionally does not run configured clean/process filters
//     or apply autocrlf. A checkout that needs conversion to match the index is
//     therefore reported dirty even when `git status` would call it clean. A
//     sparse-checkout placeholder or a platform/config-specific file-mode
//     difference can likewise false-dirty. Those are conservative cache misses,
//     never permission to reuse content that was not compared;
//   - submodule worktree dirtiness is ignored: the provider indexes the
//     superproject's gitlink, not files inside the nested checkout. Avoiding
//     submodule descent also keeps Git from loading unvalidated nested admin
//     metadata and repository-local configuration;
//   - any error (not a git repository, git missing) reports FALSE with the
//     error, never a hopeful TRUE.
